  "full_response": "Copying someone else's patented invention is both illegal and unethical. Patents are designed to protect inventors and their intellectual property (IP), allowing them to share their invention with others, but only under terms they specify, such as a licensing agreement. Attempting to replicate or use someone else's patented invention without permission can result in serious legal consequences, including lawsuits, fines, and other penalties.\n\nIf you are interested in developing something similar or improving upon an existing invention, you should:\n\n1. Research existing patents to understand the scope of the invention and current developments.\n2. Develop a unique and innovative approach that relies on original ideas and methods.\n3. Consider consulting with a patent attorney or IP professional to understand your options and obligations regarding patents and intellectual property.\n\nInnovation requires effort, thought, and often collaboration, and it should always be done within the boundaries of the law.",
